---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const sections = [
  {
    label: "01",
    title: "Overview",
    body: "tzheng.dev is built as a personal engineering system, not only a resume page. The public homepage keeps the original cyber portfolio feeling, while the writing and project layers are managed through structured content. The goal is simple: a recruiter can scan quickly, and an engineer can open the deeper pages and see how the site is actually designed."
  },
  {
    label: "02",
    title: "Design Principles",
    body: "The visual language is dark, technical, and premium, but it stays readable. The homepage uses a cinematic hero, glass navigation, terminal motion, project cards, soft grid lines, and restrained neon accents. The design avoids turning every section into a demo. Important content still has to be easy to read on a phone, a laptop, or a recruiter screen."
  },
  {
    label: "03",
    title: "Interface System",
    body: "The interface is organized around repeatable patterns: hero actions, project case cards, chips, metadata rows, timeline cards, note cards, command palette links, and footer system signals. The homepage keeps the original custom HTML/CSS/JavaScript because it carries the strongest visual identity. Astro serves it as raw HTML so the original global animation system is not broken by component scoping."
  },
  {
    label: "04",
    title: "Content Architecture",
    body: "Articles and projects are no longer treated as long-term page fragments. They live as Markdown/MDX files in content collections, with frontmatter for title, date, tags, category, featured state, status, reading time, stack, links, and summaries. This makes the writing system easier to grow: a new article is a new content file, not a risky edit inside a large homepage."
  },
  {
    label: "05",
    title: "Publishing Workflow",
    body: "The site uses a Git-based publishing workflow. Code, content, and configuration are committed to GitHub, then Cloudflare Pages builds the static output with npm run build and publishes the dist directory. That gives the site a clear history: changes are reviewable, reversible, and tied to commits instead of manual uploads."
  },
  {
    label: "06",
    title: "Security & Privacy Model",
    body: "The public site is static-first. There is no public password-only admin panel, no unnecessary database, and no server session layer for content that can be safely versioned as files. The site is delivered through Cloudflare Edge over HTTPS. It is designed without intentional advertising pixels or invasive tracking. The main security idea is to keep the public attack surface small."
  },
  {
    label: "07",
    title: "Performance & Discoverability",
    body: "Astro generates static routes for the homepage, article pages, project pages, RSS, and sitemap output. This keeps delivery fast and simple. Articles can have SEO metadata, Open Graph previews, readable URLs, tags, and table-of-contents support. Performance is treated as part of the design system: motion should feel smooth, but the site should not become heavy just to look technical."
  },
  {
    label: "08",
    title: "Trade-offs",
    body: "The site deliberately does not run a full backend today. That means there is no online database editor or public login dashboard yet. The trade-off is worth it for this stage: fewer moving parts, easier deployment, safer public surface, and content that stays portable. A Git-based CMS can be added later without changing the static-first model."
  },
  {
    label: "09",
    title: "Roadmap",
    body: "Next steps include richer MDX components, tag archives, better article search, project case-study pages, improved Open Graph images, privacy-respecting analytics, accessibility passes, and an optional Git-based CMS for editing content through a UI while still committing changes back to the repository."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title="System Design | tzheng.dev" description="How tzheng.dev is designed as a static-first engineering portfolio, content system, and Cloudflare Pages deployment.">
  <main class="shell">
    <section>
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>behind this site</span>
        <h2>System Design.</h2>
        <p>A clear look at the architecture, content model, publishing workflow, security posture, and trade-offs behind tzheng.dev.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="card featured">
          <span class="chip">static-first</span>
          <h3>Portfolio, writing system, and engineering record.</h3>
          <p>The homepage stays visually expressive, while articles and projects move into a structured content system. That lets the site feel polished on the surface and still be maintainable underneath.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card compact">
          <span class="chip">Cloudflare Pages</span>
          <h3>Git push to static deployment.</h3>
          <p>Changes go through GitHub, Cloudflare builds the Astro site, and the generated static files are served at the edge.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">architecture notes</span>
        <h2>Nine-Part Breakdown.</h2>
        <p>The system is intentionally simple where simplicity helps security and maintenance, and structured where the site needs to grow.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        {sections.map((item, index) => (
          <article class:list={["card", index % 3 === 0 && "featured", index % 3 !== 0 && "compact"]}>
            <span class="chip">{item.label}</span>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
